# .dev.vars.example — copy to .dev.vars (git-ignored; never commit the real file).
#
# Read by the Cloudflare Vite plugin that @astrojs/cloudflare v14 wires into
# `astro dev`, so these reach the worker the same way a `wrangler secret` does in
# production. NOT `.env.local`: only PUBLIC_-prefixed vars are inlined into the
# bundle, so a server secret that lives only in `.env.local` is `undefined` at
# request time on Workers (RUNBOOK §4.9). Not hot-reloaded either — restart the
# dev server after every edit (§4.2).
#
# Cloudflare Access cannot gate localhost, so the admin identity is synthesized
# here instead.

ADMIN_API_TOKEN="dev-local-token"        # echoed as x-admin-token on admin writes (defense-in-depth)
DEV_ADMIN_EMAIL="info@salongnovo.se"     # stands in for the Access identity in dev (source:"dev")

# These two look like decoration. They are load-bearing, and the trap is timed
# (RUNBOOK §4.3): `wrangler.toml`'s [vars] also flow into local dev, so the
# moment the real Access values are written there, every local /admin request
# starts 403ing with `no_token` — and with DEV_ADMIN_EMAIL also set, the
# coexistence tripwire throws first. `.dev.vars` WINS over wrangler.toml [vars]
# locally, so pinning both to "" here is what keeps the dev admin alive across
# that step. An empty string reads as UNSET everywhere in the auth code
# ("set" means truthy-after-trim, never a presence check), which is what makes
# these two lines prevent an outage rather than cause one.
ACCESS_AUD=""
ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN=""
